# 2. Local Traffic vs. Routed Traffic
The Windows routing table was inspected using:
```cmd
route print
```
The default route beginning with:
```text
0.0.0.0    0.0.0.0
```
identified the route used when Windows does not have a more specific path to a destination.
Traceroute was then used to compare local and Internet traffic:
```cmd
tracert 192.168.50.20
```
WS01 appeared immediately as the first destination because AC02 and WS01 are on the same subnet.
An external destination was then tested:
```cmd
tracert 8.8.8.8
```
The first hop was:
```text
192.168.50.1
```
followed by additional Internet routing hops before reaching Google's DNS infrastructure.
This demonstrated the difference between:
```text
Local destination
AC02 → WS01
```
and:
```text
Remote destination
AC02 → Default Gateway → Internet → Destination
```
A host does not need to send traffic through its default gateway when the destination is directly reachable on its local subnet.
---
# 3. ARP and the Neighbor Table
On AC02:
```cmd
arp -a
```
was used to inspect the local ARP cache.
After generating traffic:
```cmd
ping 192.168.50.20
```
WS01 appeared in the ARP table with an associated MAC address.
This demonstrated the relationship between Layer 3 IP addressing and Layer 2 Ethernet addressing.
Conceptually:
```text
192.168.50.20
      ↓ ARP
MAC address
```
For communication with another system on the local Ethernet network, the sending host needs to determine which MAC address corresponds to the destination IP address.
