# 8. Controlled SSH Firewall Failure
A simulated support incident was created:
> UBV01 is online, but users cannot connect to it over SSH.
Before introducing the failure, SSH connectivity was confirmed to work normally.
On UBV01, firewall rules were inspected:
```bash
sudo ufw status numbered
```
A temporary deny rule was inserted ahead of the existing SSH allow rule:
```bash
sudo ufw insert 1 deny 22/tcp
```
The existing SSH session was intentionally kept open to maintain an administrative recovery path.
From AC02, UBV01 remained reachable by IP using ICMP.
However:
```powershell
Test-NetConnection 192.168.50.219 -Port 22
```
failed.
The resulting condition was:
```text
UBV01 powered on?        Yes
Network reachable?       Yes
ICMP working?            Yes
SSH TCP/22 reachable?    No
```
---

# 9. Diagnosing the SSH Failure
From the existing administrative SSH session, the SSH service was checked:
```bash
sudo systemctl status ssh
```
The service remained:
```text
active (running)
```
Port 22 was inspected:
```bash
sudo ss -tlnp | grep ':22'
```
SSH was still listening locally.
Firewall rules were then inspected:
```bash
sudo ufw status numbered
```
The evidence showed:
```text
Host responds to ping       ✓
SSH service running         ✓
Port 22 listening locally   ✓
Remote TCP/22 connection    ✗
Firewall DENY rule          FOUND
```
The failure was therefore isolated to the firewall rather than:
- Physical connectivity
- IP addressing
- Routing
- UBV01 being offline
- SSH service failure
The temporary deny rule was removed:
```bash
sudo ufw delete 1
```
After verifying the firewall configuration, TCP/22 was tested again from AC02:
```powershell
Test-NetConnection 192.168.50.219 -Port 22
```
Result:
```text
TcpTestSucceeded : True
```
SSH connectivity had been restored.
