# 12. DNS Packet Capture
A new Wireshark capture was started using:
```text
dns
```
OPS01 explicitly queried WS01:
```bash
nslookup ws01.blackwood.local 192.168.50.20
```
Wireshark captured the DNS query and response.
The query used:
```text
Source Port:      37994
Destination Port: 53
```
The response reversed those ports:
```text
Source Port:      53
Destination Port: 37994
```
Port 53 represented the DNS service, while `37994` was a temporary client-side port used for the request.
The DNS query requested the A record for:
```text
ws01.blackwood.local
```
and WS01 returned:
```text
192.168.50.20
```
This provided a packet-level view of DNS resolution:
```text
OPS01
  │
  │ "What IPv4 address belongs to
  │  ws01.blackwood.local?"
  ▼
WS01 :53
  │
  │ "192.168.50.20"
  ▼
OPS01
```
---

# 13. TCP Three-Way Handshake
Wireshark was then filtered for SSH traffic:
```text
tcp.port == 22
```
A new SSH connection was initiated from OPS01 to UBV01:
```bash
ssh labadmin@192.168.50.219
```
At the beginning of the connection, the TCP three-way handshake was identified:
```text
OPS01                      UBV01
  │                          │
  │──── SYN ────────────────>│
  │<─── SYN, ACK ────────────│
  │──── ACK ────────────────>│
  │                          │
  │    Connection ready      │
```
This demonstrated how TCP establishes a connection before the application protocol begins communicating.
The client connected from a temporary high-numbered port to:
```text
TCP/22
```
on UBV01.
After the handshake, SSH traffic could be observed, but the contents of the remote terminal session were protected by SSH encryption.
Packet capture could still reveal metadata such as:
- Source and destination IP addresses
- Source and destination ports
- Packet sizes
- Timing
- TCP flags
- Connection establishment
but not simply expose the SSH password or terminal commands as plaintext.
