# 14. Capturing ARP
A final Wireshark capture used:
```text
arp
```
The existing neighbor entry for WS01 was removed from OPS01:

```bash
sudo ip neigh del 192.168.50.20 dev eno1
```
Traffic was then generated:
```bash
ping -c 1 192.168.50.20
```
Wireshark captured an ARP broadcast asking which MAC address owned:
```text
192.168.50.20
```
The request was sent to the Ethernet broadcast destination.
Conceptually:
```text
OPS01
  │
  │ Broadcast:
  │ "Who has 192.168.50.20?"
  ▼
Local Ethernet Segment
WS01 / virtual network
  │
  │ ARP Reply:
  │ "192.168.50.20 is at [MAC]"
  ▼
OPS01
```
After receiving the response, OPS01 could again associate WS01's IP address with the appropriate MAC address in its neighbor table.
This connected several networking concepts:
```text
Destination IP
      ↓
Determine whether destination is local
      ↓
ARP for local destination
      ↓
Resolve IP to MAC address
      ↓
Ethernet frame delivery
```
