# 15. Troubleshooting Challenge — IP vs. DNS
A hypothetical support ticket was analyzed:
> The Operations share works using `\\192.168.50.20\Operations`, but `\\WS01\Operations` fails.
Available evidence:
```text
ping 192.168.50.20
SUCCESS
Test-NetConnection 192.168.50.20 -Port 445
TRUE
\\192.168.50.20\Operations
WORKS
nslookup ws01.blackwood.local
FAILS
```
The evidence allows several possibilities to be eliminated.

```text
Basic IP connectivity       Working
WS01 reachable              Working
TCP/445                     Working
SMB share                   Working
Name resolution             Failing
```
The next area to investigate would therefore be DNS/name resolution.
An important troubleshooting distinction was identified:
**Identifying the failing system is not the same as identifying its root cause.**
The evidence supports the conclusion that name resolution is failing, but additional evidence would be required before claiming a specific DNS configuration problem.
---

# 16. Explicit DNS Server Testing
The following command was discussed as a way to narrow DNS troubleshooting:
```cmd
nslookup ws01.blackwood.local 192.168.50.20
```
The command explicitly tells `nslookup` which DNS server to query.
This can distinguish between situations such as:
```text
Normal DNS lookup       FAIL
Direct query to WS01    SUCCESS
```
which could suggest a client DNS configuration issue, versus:
```text
Normal DNS lookup       FAIL
Direct query to WS01    FAIL
```
which would justify further investigation of the DNS service, DNS records, firewall configuration, or related server-side issues.
This demonstrated a core troubleshooting principle:
> Change or isolate one variable at a time and use the result to determine the next test.
Rather than immediately guessing the root cause, each command should answer a specific diagnostic question.
---

# 17. Network Troubleshooting Workflow
The exercises developed the following troubleshooting model:
```text
Physical / Link
      ↓
IP Addressing
      ↓
Local vs. Routed Destination
      ↓
Routing / Gateway
      ↓
DNS / Name Resolution
      ↓
Port Reachability
      ↓
Firewall
      ↓
Service
      ↓
Authentication / Application
```
Not every incident requires testing every layer in this exact order.
Instead, existing evidence should be used to eliminate working components and focus testing on the most likely failing area.
For example:
```text
Can ping server              ✓
Can connect to TCP/445       ✓
Can access SMB using IP      ✓
Cannot access SMB by name    ✗
```
strongly shifts the investigation toward name resolution rather than physical connectivity or SMB.
